WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.525
Suppose worker B crashes.

00:00:01.525 --> 00:00:06.525
Until a health check detects the failure, the
balancer may still send it new work.

00:00:06.525 --> 00:00:10.306
A failed probe is evidence of a problem, not proof
of a crash.

00:00:10.306 --> 00:00:14.148
Overload, a dependency, or the network can cause a
timeout too.

00:00:14.148 --> 00:00:20.063
Production systems often use multiple observations
to avoid rapidly adding and removing a worker.

00:00:20.067 --> 00:00:23.818
For a rolling deployment, stop giving one worker new
requests.

00:00:23.818 --> 00:00:26.420
Let existing work finish within a deadline.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:32.046
Deploy the new version, wait for readiness, then
restore the worker and move to the next one.

00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:34.467
The remaining pool needs spare capacity.

00:00:34.467 --> 00:00:39.973
Old and new code coexist, so interfaces and database
schema changes must remain compatible.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:45.390
A blue-green deployment prepares another pool and
switches new traffic after verification.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.007
It consumes extra capacity.

00:00:47.007 --> 00:00:50.660
The switch does not magically move every existing
connection.

00:00:50.660 --> 00:00:53.594
A rollback also requires compatible data changes.

00:00:53.594 --> 00:00:58.325
Ask what happens to a request that was accepted just
before the traffic switch.

00:00:58.333 --> 00:01:02.736
A healthy worker pool cannot compensate for a single
failed entry point.

00:01:02.736 --> 00:01:05.672
Redundant balancers address that failure domain.

00:01:05.672 --> 00:01:08.057
A second region addresses a larger one.

00:01:08.057 --> 00:01:12.888
Retries need limits and backoff, because excessive
retries can worsen overload.

00:01:12.888 --> 00:01:18.881
For operations such as payments, use idempotency so
retrying a request does not repeat its effect.
